./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2954:The gating waist question from #132 is resolved, decorrelated, in our favour: **it closes as labour, no wall.** The mechanism is clean and classical — for a 3-layer chain, spectrally decompose (SVD) the deep layer, which turns the loss into exactly the corner-integral form we already have a banked finiteness engine for; an exact integer identity makes the resulting threshold land on `½·minAdm` on the nose. The key reframe: **3-layer chains (including the problematic waists) are best handled as a direct base case, not by the peeling step** — which dissolves #132's `(2,1,2)` obstruction entirely (a base case is never peeled, so it needs no "good end"). So the clean overall shape is: peel the good end of any ≥4-layer chain (always exists) down to a 3-layer leaf, then discharge the leaf directly by the SVD engine. **What this means for you, plainly:** after a long, careful decorrelated sweep, **the entire off-sector of the native (□) discharge is now wall-free** — every sub-question (the corner-shrink primitive, the deeper-cut admissibility, the C_hle finiteness, the wide-chain/waist regime) has resolved to labour. Honestly, it is a *large* construction — the off-sector alone is now a two-track build (a head-split "good-end" peel for the recursion + an SVD base case for 3-layer leaves), on top of the base/step/compose scaffolding — but that is the price of the operator-chosen "build it, don't cite it" native route (#97), and there is no wall in it. I'm now shifting from de-risking to building: the head-split step piece (long de-risked) goes to a formaliser now; the SVD base case (one substantial new "Wishart/Weyl density" brick, classical maths) follows; and I'm pinning the recursion wiring (good-end orientation) with one more short combinatorial check. No decision pending; flagging the scale and the all-clear.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2969:Per your scheduling note, I reorganized the (□) endgame from the serial D→F→base chain into parallel worktree lanes (Base-Cayley front-loaded, plus D-A, F, the D-C corollary, and an off-critical-path eigen-coordinate Mathlib-gap scout), pre-staged the #108 mint wiring (verified end-to-end: `DecoratedDescent → driver → (□) → unconditional headline` typechecks green), and built a durable interface tracker (`endgame-lanes.md`) since the parallel builds need tight interface discipline. **The notable outcome:** both #156 base-case tides, at their mandatory pre-build checkpoint, INDEPENDENTLY found that the 3-width base-case finiteness is *already proved* — `routeMBoxThresholdFinite_mnp` (unconditional over all widths, clean-three, and already the load-bearing R1-UPPER atom of our L2 interface). I verified it myself (faithful reshape to the box integral, threshold `= ½·minAdm` by `rfl`, no waist-excluding hypothesis, no `sorry`). So the whole SVD/Cayley waist construction the certs had scoped — a heavy build resting on an eigenvalue-density result Mathlib lacks — was **redundant**: the certs designed it without knowing `_mnp` closed the waist via a different banked route (a radial-Schur recursion). **What this means for you, plainly:** two lanes stood down with zero wasted Lean, the endgame is one genuine brick lighter than believed, and the "audit banked work before re-deriving" discipline (same family as the earlier Wall-C over-scope) paid off exactly as intended — decorrelated tides + a controller verify caught it at the checkpoint. No decision pending. The residual it leaves is item #137.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2978:Concrete recurrence of the #129 hazard. I had wired Brick D-A into the aggregator + axiom-check (uncommitted) and then launched a new formaliser tide; that tide's startup `git reset --hard origin/expedition/aoyagi-full` executed against the *main* checkout's HEAD (shared `.git`, isolation not fully taking), discarding my uncommitted tracked-file edits (the new source files, being untracked, survived). I diagnosed it from the reflog (`HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/…`), re-wired, green-gated, and landed cleanly — zero lasting harm. **Reinforced standing practice:** commit *and push* the controller's state before launching any sub-agent, and never hold uncommitted tracked changes while one is running; on integration, `git add` explicit paths only (never `-A`, since a flaky tide may drop files in main). This is the same harness/config question #129 raised — whether background sub-agents should get real worktree isolation here — now with a concrete (harmless) instance. Not blocking; flagging for the eventual isolation decision.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2984:Brick F2a (`measurableEigenvalues₀`: the sorted eigenvalues of a measurable Hermitian family are measurable in the parameter — Vieta-evaluation + Lusin–Souslin, contour-free, no root-continuity) is **Proved, sorry-free, axiom-clean** `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]` (I re-verified with a force-fresh `#print axioms`, not just the tide's report), self-reviewed FIDELITY+SOUNDNESS PASS (Codex-corroborated). It's the first rung of the measurable-eigendecomposition ladder and is **doubly load-bearing**: conjunct (i) of Brick F's primitive AND the source of the plumbing's `hGmeas` good-set measurability. Landed on canonical. **Process note (for your awareness):** the tide ran `git commit --amend` (to fix a non-load-bearing docstring line its reviewer flagged), which tripped the harness's "[Git Destructive]" security heuristic (it couldn't see a prior in-session agent commit). I did **not** act on the report blind — I verified the amend rewrote only the tide's *own* new commit (parent = a real canonical ancestor, diff = the one new module file, 201 insertions, nothing else touched) before integrating. Benign; the heuristic false-positived on a subagent-internal amend. No harm. Flagging as a recurring harness/isolation friction point (companion to #129/#139) — the amend heuristic doesn't see subagent-internal commits, so tide self-amends will keep tripping it; the controller-verifies-before-integrating discipline handles it.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2987:Transparency + a process-health note. In fanning out the head-split's analytic crux (GLUE-2 = `headSplit_pivotDom`) and its measure-plumbing, two tides surfaced genuine issues in what I had locked, *before* sinking build budget — exactly the verify-before-build discipline. **(1) My architecture-A lock was unsound** (a controller error): I told the GLUE-2 tide to prove finiteness by "drop the corank + D-B codim," claiming hpiv covered the threshold. It doesn't — I conflated `minAdm(redChain)/2` with `minAdm(M)/2`; the binding-cut identity `minAdm(M)=ab+minAdm(redChain)` means dropping the corank loses exactly the `ab/2` the corank *regularizes*, so the route undershoots the needed threshold and the statement is false-by-that-route in a real gap regime (numerically witnessed at (3,3,3), c'∈(3,3.5)). The tide caught it, I confirmed and corrected: keep the corank, isolate the honest crux `pivotDom_finiteness : (RHS<⊤)→(LHS<⊤)` as one sorry; the non-pointwise cross-term drop is irreducible (architectures A and B collapse to it). **(2) The head-split stub was under-constrained**: the plumbing tide found `headSplit_domination` needs two scope hypotheses the certs already imply but the stub omitted — `ε' ≤ ε/√(M₁M₂)` (the shell⊆good containment threshold, S1 cert §B.3) and `j < r` strict (the saturated shell `j=r` is a separate arity-IH branch, final-assembly cert). Both added; I own the stub edit + verifying the `j=r` branch isn't dropped at integration. **Plainly:** no wrong build resulted from either — the decorrelated tide-level verification (numeric witness for #1, D-C-consumption analysis for #2) is doing what the charter asks, catching my imprecision at the design boundary rather than 600 lines in. Recording that I made the arch-A error (not the tide) in the interest of an honest ledger; the correction cost one message round-trip, not a build.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2990:Companion to #129/#139/#141, now more serious. A formaliser tide I launched with `isolation: worktree` (the F-brick reconciliation) intermittently ran in the CONTROLLER'S MAIN checkout instead of its worktree: it `git reset --hard origin/…` (wiping my uncommitted synthesis ledger note twice) and `git checkout … -- <3 modules>` (staging files in main), and — since its brief said "commit periodically" (a guard I add for worktree tides) — was on track to commit reconcile progress directly to `expedition/aoyagi-full`. I caught it at "before editing" (via `git status` showing staged files in main + reflog showing the reset), STOPPED it before any edit/commit, and confirmed canonical was uncorrupted (HEAD unmoved, the checked-out modules were unedited originals). No harm, but only because I was watching. **Mitigations now standing:** (1) commit+push the controller's state before launching ANY tide, and never hold uncommitted tracked-file edits while one starts (its startup `git reset` can fire late, after a launch-when-clean — so even that isn't fully safe); (2) every tide brief now carries an **isolation self-check**: `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` must contain `.claude/worktrees`, else STOP and report — so a mis-isolated tide self-aborts instead of colliding; (3) task-store updates (which survive resets) for anything I need durable while tides run. **For you:** this is a real harness/config question — background `isolation: worktree` is not reliable here (it works for most tides, fails intermittently for some), and a mis-isolated tide with git-write instructions is a live risk to the integration branch. Worth a deliberate fix (guaranteed isolation, or a hook that refuses tide git-writes outside `.claude/worktrees`) rather than the controller-vigilance workaround. It has cost zero canonical damage so far, but it's the highest-friction recurring item in the expedition.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2993:Worse manifestation of #129/#139/#141/#143. A freshly-launched helper tide (mechanical RHS≠0/uzero) — despite an isolation SELF-CHECK guard in its brief instructing it to abort if not in a worktree — ran its startup `git reset --hard origin/genm-sj5-pivotdom` + `git checkout -b genm-sj5-pivotdom-fill` **in the controller's MAIN checkout**, moving main's HEAD off `expedition/aoyagi-full` onto a tide branch. My next controller commit (a tracker re-flush) then landed on that wrong branch, and the push to `expedition/aoyagi-full` was correctly rejected (non-fast-forward) — which is how I caught it. **No damage to canonical:** Brick F was already committed+pushed to `origin/expedition/aoyagi-full` (@5e114405); I recovered by `git checkout -f expedition/aoyagi-full` + `git reset --hard origin/…` (Brick F intact), deleted the stray branch, and re-applied the tracker on the correct line. The helper's own work is safe in its worktree. **Why the guard failed:** the tide appears to run its `git reset` at startup before/around the self-check, so a brief-level guard can't reliably prevent it — the reset is baked into the launch. **This is no longer a minor annoyance:** an auto-launched tide with git-write behavior in the shared main checkout can silently move the integration branch; only the non-ff push rejection + controller vigilance caught it. **Recommendation (repeating, now stronger): a harness-level fix is needed** — either guaranteed worktree isolation for `isolation: worktree` agents, or a pre-push/pre-reset hook that refuses git-writes to `expedition/aoyagi-full` from anything running outside `.claude/worktrees`. Until then my standing practice is: after every tide launch, `git branch --show-current` must read `expedition/aoyagi-full`; commit+push controller edits immediately; never hold uncommitted tracked edits. Zero canonical damage across all four incidents, but the vigilance cost is now material and the failure mode is a branch move, not just a file revert.
./expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/discuss-at-close.md:2996:Downgrade + concrete fix for #144. The helper tide reported the mechanism: at startup it ran `git reset --hard origin/genm-sj5-pivotdom`. The harness DOES set the agent's cwd to its worktree — but the explicit `cd <absolute-repo-root>` overrode it, so the `git reset` executed in the MAIN checkout (which was on expedition/aoyagi-full), moving the branch. So the "flaky isolation" is not fundamentally broken: it is **tides running `cd <repo-root>` (or any absolute repo path) in their bash commands**, which lands their git/build in main. The self-check guard (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`) doesn't help because the `cd` happens in the same breath as the git op. **The fix is concrete:** (1) controller-side (now standing) — every tide brief forbids `cd` to the repo root / absolute repo paths; run all git/build from the worktree cwd (relative paths), verify `pwd` is under `.claude/worktrees/` before any git op. I've reminded the two live git-writing tides. (2) harness-side (the real fix, for your consideration) — either make `isolation: worktree` agents' git refuse writes to a checkout that isn't their worktree, or a pre-commit/pre-reset hook rejecting operations on `expedition/aoyagi-full` from outside `.claude/worktrees/`. **Net:** the incidents were a preventable usage error (tide `cd`), not an unavoidable harness defect — good news; it means the standing controller practice (forbid `cd` in briefs + branch-verify after launches) should eliminate recurrence, and the operator has a clean harness-hardening target if desired. Zero canonical damage across all incidents.
